# Dataplane interface: the one carrying subscriber traffic. Names the device
# handed to VPP and chooses the NUMA node for the workers.
#
# EMPTY ON PURPOSE, AND EMPTY NO LONGER MEANS NO CARD (#378). The box claims
# every PCI network card it has except one reserved for Linux - the management
# network it was installed with - and never the last one. So a fresh install
# forwards without being told anything, and 'show hardware' says which cards
# it took and why.
#
# Set this to override that: the box then claims the card named here and no
# other, which is worth doing on a machine with several cards where only one
# carries subscriber traffic. Restart prisma-routing-tune and vpp afterwards.
#
# It used to ship as "eth0", which does not exist on a Debian 12 box with
# predictable interface names - so the tuner could not resolve it, generated a
# DPDK stanza with no device allowlist, and VPP took whatever NIC it found
# (#123). An allowlist is still computed for every card claimed; what changed
# is which cards are in it, never whether there is one.
PRISMA_DATAPLANE_IF=

# Subscriber target: sizes the hugepages.
PRISMA_SUBSCRIBERS=200000

# Low latency: disables deep C-states and transparent hugepages, and keeps
# the cpufreq governor on performance. Costs power, buys predictable bursts.
# Set to 0 only if the box must save energy more than it must forward.
PRISMA_LOW_LATENCY=1

# Worker cap: the most physical cores handed to VPP as forwarding workers.
#
# EMPTY ON PURPOSE, and empty means no cap - exactly what the box does today:
# every physical core on the dataplane NUMA node except the first becomes a
# worker. Changing that default would change the throughput of every installed
# box on its next boot, which is not a decision a tuning default gets to make.
#
# Why it exists: on a 32-core socket "all but the first" leaves ONE physical
# core carrying FRR, frr-reload, vtysh, prismash, sshd and VPP's main thread
# together. The main thread is not idle - bpa-expire-process takes the worker
# barrier on it once a second, and forwarding is stopped for as long as that
# thread is off the CPU. The more cores the box has, the more control-plane
# work is crowded onto the one core that must never be preempted.
#
# Set it to hand cores back. A cap below the number available is honoured; a
# cap above it does not invent workers. The cores it frees stop being isolated,
# so the scheduler can actually use them - a cap that only shortened the worker
# list would cost throughput and buy nothing.
#
# The cost is real: each worker removed is that share of forwarding capacity.
# Nothing here measures the trade, so no non-empty default is shipped.
PRISMA_MAX_WORKERS=
